A||||10||RFMike||More Brakes||||5||
Z||000000||RFMike||12-16-1999||05:53 AM||||In the recent brakes post, Standard posted a link to a sight that described installing a lower strength spring in the proportioning valve after the author had installed upgraded front brakes.  He then had balanced front and rear braking.  This is the sight:  <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/99/4/we.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/99/4/we.htm</A>  <BR>My question: has anybody done this modification?  The author declined to give the spring strength that he eventually used. Maybe a liability issue? Thanks for any responses.<BR>Mike||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||12-16-1999||06:20 AM||||I still think it would be smarter, safer and easier to simply replace the rear brakes, too.  Surely someone can figure out a way to get the parking brake to work with the rear Grand Am upgrade.  After all, we have some of the greatest Fiero minds in the world right here on our Forum!||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||12-16-1999||06:56 PM||||There will likely never be a way to make the hand brake work with the grand am caliper set.<P>do not attempt to modify the stock proportioning vavle. instead replace it with an adjustable unit. Properly adjusted there is no reason at all that you cannot stay stock in the rear, and use the grand am set in front.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierosound||12-16-1999||07:10 PM||||Theogre... I agree, it is dangerous to try to modify the caliper.  But, later versions of the Grand AM had 4 wheel disc brakes.  Hasn't anyone checked to see if these rear calipers will work on a Fiero??||||reg||10
Z||000004||william_thorniii||12-16-1999||07:18 PM||||fierosound,<P>I think that the problem with that idea is that (I think) they also upgraded the disk to be vented and therefore will not fit our non-vented calipers.  I could be wrong, but I thought that was the hitch.  I have heard though that some S-10's had a similar disk with a RWD and rear parking brake.  Perhaps this will work?<P>By the way usually 80-20 braking is standard.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000005||RFMike||12-16-1999||07:50 PM||||M-Man:  It is not smarter to disable the parking brake on a stick, and in fact is illegal.<BR>Ogre, any ideas where to get an adjustable p-valve?  I was told at NAPA they don't sell them for the FIero.<BR>F-Sound: this isn't a caliper adjustment.  Read the link above.<BR>William_T3: this is a method to upgrade the front brakes, and modify the amount of hydrostatic pressure to optimize the front/rear ratio.  That is what I meant when I said balanced.  <BR>Thanks for the responses.  Looks like I need to look for an aftermarket valve.<BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phil||12-16-1999||09:19 PM||||most speed shops sell adjustable p valves about $65 I think||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||12-16-1999||11:57 PM||||A lot of street rodders use adjustable proportioning valves. If done right, these work good. Give Engineered Components, Inc. (ECI) a call. They specialize in street rod brakes and will be able to fix you up. I've bought stuff from them before and they are super helpful. All they do are brakes and nothing else, so they are brake experts. Call 860-872-7046.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||12-17-1999||01:45 AM||||RFMike-I didn't say anything about disabling the parking brake, especially on the stick.  I don't think it would make much of a difference on the auto though unless you made a habit of parking on a hill.  (I live in Indiana-flat like a 10 year old boy.)  I figured that someone could find a way to make the p-brake work.  I guess not.||||reg||1
Z||000009||HarryG||12-17-1999||12:49 PM||||RFMike,<BR>If you decide to change the spring in your stock P-valve, check first to see what size is in there.  After I read the brake article in WebElectric, I checked our local boneyard. They had an 86 and an 87. The springs in those two valves were different sizes. Maybe Pontiac's specs changed or they were from different suppliers?<BR>Harry   ||||reg||1
Z||000010||RFMike||12-18-1999||06:25 AM||||Thanks for the responses, guys.  I will let you know what I come up with.<BR>Mike||||reg||7
